WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.267
Discuss this and more

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.235
with the former U.S.

00:00:02.235 --> 00:00:04.204
Treasury secretary, Larry Summers.

00:00:04.204 --> 00:00:04.471
Mr.

00:00:04.471 --> 00:00:06.373
Secretary, thanks so much for joining us.

00:00:06.373 --> 00:00:08.108
Did the Fed get it right today

00:00:08.108 --> 00:00:11.611
with this quarter point hike?

00:00:13.146 --> 00:00:15.281
I think of the

00:00:15.281 --> 00:00:17.017
difficult choices there were.

00:00:17.017 --> 00:00:19.152
This was the right choice.

00:00:19.652 --> 00:00:22.155
If the Fed had stopped raising

00:00:22.155 --> 00:00:22.989
interest rates

00:00:22.989 --> 00:00:25.225
when it clearly had had a plan

00:00:25.592 --> 00:00:27.994
to increase interest rates,

00:00:28.328 --> 00:00:30.630
I think the risk would have been

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:33.266
that it was signaling panic and alarm.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.602
And if the Fed was that alarmed,

00:00:35.935 --> 00:00:36.603
the market

00:00:36.603 --> 00:00:38.872
and everyone else would be as well.

00:00:38.872 --> 00:00:40.540
So I think carrying through

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:42.942
was the broadly right thing to do.

00:00:42.942 --> 00:00:45.645
That was what the market expected

00:00:45.645 --> 00:00:46.746
the Fed to do,

00:00:46.746 --> 00:00:49.149
and I think it was appropriate.

00:00:49.783 --> 00:00:53.153
I think the Fed is right to be signaling

00:00:53.153 --> 00:00:56.689
enormous uncertainty going forward.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:57.991
I actually think

00:00:57.991 --> 00:00:59.993
there are two possible paths.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.729
One is that there's going to be

00:01:02.729 --> 00:01:04.431
some real durability

00:01:04.431 --> 00:01:07.434
in these banking problems and the economy

00:01:07.434 --> 00:01:10.303
is going to turn down.

00:01:10.737 --> 00:01:14.507
The other is that this will be weathered

00:01:14.808 --> 00:01:17.911
and very much contained.

00:01:18.211 --> 00:01:20.613
And what the Fed's going to need to do

00:01:20.613 --> 00:01:21.981
going forward

00:01:21.981 --> 00:01:23.116
is going to depend

00:01:23.116 --> 00:01:26.119
on which of those paths we end up

00:01:26.119 --> 00:01:29.556
on, which I don't think anybody can know.

00:01:29.823 --> 00:01:32.859
I think the Fed was right to signal

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.829
enormous uncertainty.

00:01:36.229 --> 00:01:40.667
I probably would have allowed more room

00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:44.471
for concern about inflation

00:01:44.471 --> 00:01:45.738
and left the door

00:01:45.738 --> 00:01:49.409
a bit more open to multiple rate hikes,

00:01:49.742 --> 00:01:51.778
given the strength of the recent

00:01:52.345 --> 00:01:53.980
inflation data

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:56.349
than the Fed did in their statement.

00:01:56.683 --> 00:01:58.551
But it's a very close

00:01:59.586 --> 00:02:01.688
call, and I think what they did

00:02:01.888 --> 00:02:06.392
was entirely, entirely reasonable.

00:02:06.693 --> 00:02:10.063
A lot of is going to depend upon how

00:02:10.964 --> 00:02:13.433
the regulatory authorities and the bank

00:02:13.433 --> 00:02:15.235
insurance authorities

00:02:15.235 --> 00:02:17.670
deal with the remaining bank problem

00:02:18.538 --> 00:02:20.573
out there and what happens

00:02:21.241 --> 00:02:23.610
in the banking system.

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:25.245
I don't know if you were watching,

00:02:25.245 --> 00:02:26.312
but Democratic Senator

00:02:26.312 --> 00:02:27.881
Elizabeth Warren of Massachusetts,

00:02:27.881 --> 00:02:29.449
this says Jerome Powell,

00:02:29.449 --> 00:02:31.017
the Federal Reserve chair,

00:02:31.017 --> 00:02:33.586
is doing what she calls a terrible job.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:35.722
Watch what she told our Jake TAPPER

00:02:35.722 --> 00:02:37.690
just a little while ago.

00:02:37.891 --> 00:02:40.059
He has spent five years

00:02:40.493 --> 00:02:42.295
weakening regulations

00:02:42.295 --> 00:02:44.497
over these multibillion dollar banks.

00:02:44.864 --> 00:02:45.565
I predicted

00:02:45.565 --> 00:02:46.366
five years ago

00:02:46.366 --> 00:02:48.668
the consequence of that kind of weakening

00:02:49.202 --> 00:02:51.271
would be that we would see these banks

00:02:51.471 --> 00:02:52.939
load up on risk, build

00:02:52.939 --> 00:02:54.440
their short term profits,

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:57.343
give themselves ginormous up

00:02:58.011 --> 00:03:00.146
bonuses and big salaries,

00:03:00.446 --> 00:03:01.314
and then some of those

00:03:01.314 --> 00:03:02.348
banks will explode.

00:03:02.348 --> 00:03:04.717
And that is exactly what has happened.

00:03:04.951 --> 00:03:06.052
On share panels,

00:03:06.052 --> 00:03:08.054
what she says Powell

00:03:08.054 --> 00:03:09.122
shouldn't be chairman

00:03:09.122 --> 00:03:10.123
of the Federal Reserve.

00:03:10.123 --> 00:03:11.758
Do you have concerns, Mr.

00:03:11.758 --> 00:03:15.128
Secretary, with his leadership?

00:03:16.896 --> 00:03:17.530
No, I

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:20.099
think he is a appropriate

00:03:20.633 --> 00:03:23.236
Fed Fed chair.

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:28.308
I have disagreed with Secretary Warren

00:03:28.308 --> 00:03:29.909
on many Senator

00:03:29.909 --> 00:03:32.579
Warren on many aspects of regulation.

00:03:32.912 --> 00:03:34.581
But I think she has

00:03:34.581 --> 00:03:38.651
she is on strong grounds in criticizing

00:03:38.651 --> 00:03:41.487
the Fed's oversight of Silicon

00:03:41.487 --> 00:03:42.622
Valley Bank

00:03:42.622 --> 00:03:44.457
that was not directly Chairman

00:03:44.457 --> 00:03:46.759
Powell's responsibility.

00:03:46.759 --> 00:03:49.262
And I don't think it was largely

00:03:49.262 --> 00:03:51.431
a matter of broad policies.

00:03:51.798 --> 00:03:52.232
It was

00:03:52.232 --> 00:03:55.368
a failure of supervision on the ground

00:03:56.169 --> 00:03:58.571
when a bank has no chief risk officer

00:03:58.571 --> 00:04:00.006
for nine months

00:04:00.006 --> 00:04:02.842
during a highly risky period

00:04:03.042 --> 00:04:04.143
where its deposit

00:04:04.143 --> 00:04:08.348
rates have nearly doubled in size.

00:04:08.715 --> 00:04:09.549
The authorities

00:04:09.549 --> 00:04:11.251
are supposed to do something

00:04:11.251 --> 00:04:12.852
and they didn't

00:04:12.852 --> 00:04:16.322
see what were actually quite obvious

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:19.892
risks in that institution.

00:04:19.892 --> 00:04:20.460
And I think there

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:23.896
needs to be very careful investigation

00:04:24.197 --> 00:04:26.633
of the individuals

00:04:26.633 --> 00:04:28.534
who bore responsibility

00:04:28.534 --> 00:04:31.471
directly for the supervision of the bank

00:04:31.471 --> 00:04:34.140
and those who supervise them.

00:04:34.574 --> 00:04:37.410
I do think it's an old issue.

00:04:37.677 --> 00:04:38.911
And, you know,

00:04:38.911 --> 00:04:40.046
you and I have known each other

00:04:40.046 --> 00:04:41.047
a long time, Wolf.

00:04:41.047 --> 00:04:41.948
I'm not

00:04:42.148 --> 00:04:45.084
usually on the populist side of things.

00:04:45.451 --> 00:04:47.520
But I have to say, I don't get

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:50.690
why are president of Silicon Valley banks

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:53.559
should be on the board of the

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:57.497
San Francisco Fed.

00:04:57.497 --> 00:04:59.799
And I don't get why such a person

00:05:00.033 --> 00:05:01.000
should be permitted

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:02.268
to stay on the board

00:05:02.268 --> 00:05:02.702
of the San

00:05:02.702 --> 00:05:06.572
Francisco Fed after the San Francisco

00:05:06.572 --> 00:05:10.009
Fed is involved in giving his bank

00:05:10.677 --> 00:05:13.546
very substantial warnings.

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:16.149
So I think that needs to be looked at.

00:05:16.516 --> 00:05:17.717
I think it's

00:05:17.717 --> 00:05:20.186
somebody not doing their job right.

00:05:20.486 --> 00:05:22.855
When the Fed does stress tests

00:05:23.222 --> 00:05:25.191
and the stress tests,

00:05:25.191 --> 00:05:27.260
don't consider a scenario

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:29.162
of rising interest rates.

00:05:29.162 --> 00:05:31.497
God knows in the spring of 2022,

00:05:31.497 --> 00:05:32.799
it was obvious

00:05:32.799 --> 00:05:34.200
that that was the major

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:36.269
risk for the economy,

00:05:36.269 --> 00:05:38.338
but not according to the stress tests

00:05:38.338 --> 00:05:39.939
the Fed was using.

00:05:39.939 --> 00:05:40.840
That actually,

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:42.875
I think, is the more telling point.

00:05:43.142 --> 00:05:45.578
Yes, it's true.

00:05:45.578 --> 00:05:47.347
Senator Warren is right.

00:05:47.347 --> 00:05:50.450
That legislation that exempted

00:05:50.817 --> 00:05:51.951
smaller banks

00:05:51.951 --> 00:05:53.386
like Silicon Valley Bank

00:05:53.386 --> 00:05:54.120
from the stress

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:57.657
tests was bad legislation.

00:05:57.990 --> 00:05:59.559
But even if it had been subject

00:05:59.559 --> 00:06:01.260
to the stress tests,

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:04.030
the scenario that got them isn't

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:08.234
part of the stress testing system.

00:06:08.468 --> 00:06:10.236
So I think there is a lot

00:06:10.236 --> 00:06:11.971
that needs to be

00:06:11.971 --> 00:06:14.707
looked at in the way that Fed

00:06:14.941 --> 00:06:18.010
the Fed does supervision.

00:06:18.311 --> 00:06:20.079
I think that

00:06:20.079 --> 00:06:20.713
the person

00:06:20.713 --> 00:06:22.482
who's really got that responsible

00:06:22.482 --> 00:06:25.918
polity now, Michael Barr, is someone

00:06:25.918 --> 00:06:30.423
who has a long tradition, a long history

00:06:30.423 --> 00:06:32.191
of being very concerned

00:06:32.191 --> 00:06:34.193
about financial access.

00:06:34.193 --> 00:06:37.797
And I expect that he will shape things up

00:06:38.097 --> 00:06:41.267
and will correct a variety of the errors

00:06:41.567 --> 00:06:42.935
that, frankly, were made

00:06:42.935 --> 00:06:44.570
by some of ex-president

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:47.874
Trump's appointees who were involved in

00:06:48.508 --> 00:06:52.145
financial regulation and supervision.

00:06:52.779 --> 00:06:54.881
But no, I think that,

00:06:55.915 --> 00:06:57.550
if anything,

00:06:57.550 --> 00:06:59.819
Chairman Powell's biggest mistakes

00:07:00.153 --> 00:07:02.855
in his most important responsible ability

00:07:03.256 --> 00:07:03.956
were that

00:07:03.956 --> 00:07:04.390
he didn't

00:07:04.390 --> 00:07:05.925
recognize the inflation

00:07:05.925 --> 00:07:07.560
threat soon enough

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:08.227
and didn't

00:07:08.227 --> 00:07:10.129
move strongly enough

00:07:10.129 --> 00:07:12.098
to counter that threat.

00:07:12.098 --> 00:07:14.133
And Senator Warren

00:07:14.133 --> 00:07:16.569
was even less concerned, and

00:07:17.670 --> 00:07:20.506
the very progressives were even less

00:07:20.506 --> 00:07:24.577
concerned about inflation than he was.

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:26.345
So I think

00:07:26.345 --> 00:07:29.515
those criticisms are quite unfair.

00:07:29.549 --> 00:07:31.884
I do think that there is a lot

00:07:31.884 --> 00:07:33.986
to worry about in the regulatory area.

00:07:34.153 --> 00:07:34.921
All right, Mr.

00:07:34.921 --> 00:07:36.789
Secretary, as usual, thank you so much.

